A Kaggle kernel runs one uploaded file, so a sysone job on Kaggle is the runner with the job’s folder embedded in it: kaggle kernels push uploads that file with a kernel-metadata.json (the accelerator, internet on, the datasets to mount), and the kernel unpacks the job into /kaggle/working/sysone_job and runs it. Its log is followed live (kaggle kernels logs -f, in a background process writing the job’s log here), its status polled (kaggle kernels status), and at the end kaggle kernels output brings outputs.tar.gz back. The runner stops a job at its max_hours and Kaggle stops the kernel half an hour later (KAGGLE_GRACE), so a job that runs long still packs its outputs; a kernel Kaggle stops first keeps the files it wrote, but not the tarball.
